The fix is in src/hooks/useLiveJewellery.ts. Compute the catalogue once per query-data reference instead of on every hook call. The smallest change, and the one I measured, is a module-level cache shared by all 16 consumers:

const catalogueCache = new WeakMap<ShopifyProductNode[], JewelPiece[]>();
const cachedCatalogue = (data: ShopifyProductNode[]) => {
  let hit = catalogueCache.get(data);
  if (!hit) { hit = liveCatalogue(data); catalogueCache.set(data, hit); }
  return hit;
};
// in useLiveJewellery, line 305:
return { jewellery: cachedCatalogue(data), isLive: true, isLoading: false };

React Query's structural sharing keeps `data` referentially stable across renders and unchanged 60 s refetches, so the mapping runs only when the catalogue actually changes. The `jewellery` identity also stays stable, so JewelDetailNext's piece, images, pairings and similar memos stop recomputing.

Alternatives:
- `useMemo(() => (data?.length ? liveCatalogue(data) : null), [data])`. It must be placed before the early return at line 304 to follow the rules of hooks, and it still maps once per mounted consumer.
- `select: liveCatalogue` on the useQuery call. This also maps once per observer.

